            },
            {
              "type": "null"
            }
          ]
        },
        "binding": {
          "$ref": "adapter.schema.json#/$defs/InstanceReference"
        }
      },
      "required": [
        "scope",
        "cursor",
        "limit",
        "barrier",
        "binding"
      ],
      "additionalProperties": false,
      "description": "Each newly executed null-cursor invocation starts a fresh snapshot. If its first response was not accepted, a new null-cursor invocation may restart the scan. Replay of an already accepted page reuses the receiving owner's retained exact request and response; it does not ask a fresh adapter process to regenerate the old first page. Only a retained non-null cursor continues the previously selected native snapshot. A null barrier requests ordinary inventory only. A non-null barrier must match scope and its owner authority, and identifies a fresh owner-issued post-drain scan under continuously held native exclusion. Every continuation retains that exact challenge. Returned observations echo it unchanged; an earlier scan cannot be relabeled with a new challenge. The request data does not itself establish permission, exclusion or quiescence. The limit bounds the sum of returned top-level collection entries in objects; nested fields within one entry are not separate records. Other scalar or object output fields are fixed-shape metadata and do not count toward this limit. A continuation is an opaque receiver-issued cursor bound to the exact approved executor, scope, query and one stable native observation snapshot. The receiver verifies those bindings on every continuation. Incomplete coverage requires a non-null cursor different from the request cursor; complete coverage has no continuation. Unknown coverage is not resumable and has no continuation; an unavailable or changed snapshot is explicitly unknown and requires a new scan with cursor null. Known records may accompany unknown coverage, but cannot establish complete absence. These per-page bounds do not limit total collection extent or prove native bounded work. The exact observer binding is required and independently approved by the receiver. Every object scope equals the request scope; no aggregate or inferred subordinate scope is selected. Object handle is the logical identity within that scope; native_selector and incarnation cannot redefine a repeated handle. Historical produced_by remains the exact original attempt identity and is not rewritten to the observer. A non-null runtime observation matches the binding instance and installation digest. Logical identities occur once across the complete stable snapshot, not merely once per page. Duplicate identities, including conflicting validity or value handles, reject within or across pages. Receiver-local replay of an already accepted request and canonical response is idempotent and does not consume its records again; a changed response to that request rejects. Receivers retain indexed identities and page commitments, so total intake work is linear in accepted page and retry bytes without rescanning history. Native cursor/snapshot authentication and current permission remain receiver duties."
    },
